WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:03.085
Welcome to the Federal
Modernization Workbook's

2
00:00:03.085 --> 00:00:05.750
developer workbook, Building Defender XDR.

3
00:00:05.750 --> 00:00:09.625
This is Chapter 1: access,
and the Graph read path.

4
00:00:09.625 --> 00:00:12.775
Every check in this workbook
reads through one door.

5
00:00:12.775 --> 00:00:17.182
Before anything else, we prove the door
is the right one, in the right cloud,

6
00:00:17.182 --> 00:00:21.075
and that the people behind it hold
only what the design gave them.

7
00:00:21.075 --> 00:00:25.737
We'll start from Microsoft Learn's
commands, read how the kit declares its

8
00:00:25.737 --> 00:00:30.400
calls and checks its roles, and walk
two labs, the evidence and the dangers.

9
00:00:30.400 --> 00:00:33.499
The chapter is a draft
awaiting the owner's review,

10
00:00:33.499 --> 00:00:37.260
and the code is illustrative,
tested against fixtures.

11
00:00:41.010 --> 00:00:42.048
Why start here?

12
00:00:42.048 --> 00:00:45.857
Because every later chapter
reads through Microsoft Graph:

13
00:00:45.857 --> 00:00:49.972
incidents, advanced hunting
results and custom detection rules.

14
00:00:49.972 --> 00:00:53.531
If the session behind those
reads is in the wrong tenant,

15
00:00:53.531 --> 00:00:57.322
it still answers, and a check
that reads an empty list passes.

16
00:00:57.322 --> 00:01:01.772
So by the end of this chapter you can run
the kit's access check, read its findings,

17
00:01:01.772 --> 00:01:05.811
and say which Graph permission and
which role each later chapter needs.

18
00:01:05.811 --> 00:01:10.469
It's for the developers who maintain the
kit, the platform owner who owns this gate,

19
00:01:10.469 --> 00:01:14.450
and the security officer who
reviews who holds Defender roles.

20
00:01:18.200 --> 00:01:21.500
Start from what the guide and
Microsoft Learn already give you.

21
00:01:21.500 --> 00:01:26.067
Create the custom roles in Defender's
unified role-based access control,

22
00:01:26.067 --> 00:01:30.025
in four permission groups, assigned
to groups and data sources.

23
00:01:30.025 --> 00:01:34.050
Activate it per workload, which needs
at least Security Administrator.

24
00:01:34.050 --> 00:01:38.555
Connect the read path with Connect
M G Graph, the Global environment,

25
00:01:38.555 --> 00:01:42.738
and two scopes: security incident
read, and threat hunting read.

26
00:01:42.738 --> 00:01:44.841
Then validate with Learn's own call:

27
00:01:44.841 --> 00:01:47.887
list up to five incidents
with no permission error.

28
00:01:47.887 --> 00:01:53.051
Learn's national cloud page says GCC
uses the worldwide Graph endpoint,

29
00:01:53.051 --> 00:01:55.510
so the environment is Global.

30
00:01:59.260 --> 00:02:00.597
What does that leave to you?

31
00:02:00.597 --> 00:02:05.293
A connect line with an environment, but
a session left over from another tenant

32
00:02:05.293 --> 00:02:09.772
answers too, so the kit refuses a live
run in the wrong environment or tenant.

33
00:02:09.772 --> 00:02:13.956
Permissions page by page, but
nobody sees the whole job's needs,

34
00:02:13.956 --> 00:02:18.797
so the kit declares every call with
its permission, roles and Learn page.

35
00:02:18.797 --> 00:02:22.157
A role design in prose, but
a portal role list drifts,

36
00:02:22.157 --> 00:02:25.197
so the kit compares the
export with the design.

37
00:02:25.197 --> 00:02:27.247
And a pass on no permission error,

38
00:02:27.247 --> 00:02:30.661
so the kit makes the read one
result row and keeps going.

39
00:02:30.661 --> 00:02:34.690
The verdict: keep Learn's calls;
build the checks around them.

40
00:02:38.440 --> 00:02:39.690
Here's the call list.

41
00:02:39.690 --> 00:02:45.024
Three reads: list incidents, run a hunting
query, and list custom detection rules,

42
00:02:45.024 --> 00:02:49.065
each with the least-privileged
read permission Learn names.

43
00:02:49.065 --> 00:02:54.028
And two writes, create and update a detection
rule, with the read-write permission.

44
00:02:54.028 --> 00:02:57.327
Reads go through one function
and one of two invokers.

45
00:02:57.327 --> 00:03:01.591
The fixture invoker reads a file
and sends nothing; that's Lab 1.

46
00:03:01.591 --> 00:03:05.069
The live invoker calls Graph
on the settings file's cloud,

47
00:03:05.069 --> 00:03:09.215
after the session check, and follows
next links only on the same host.

48
00:03:09.215 --> 00:03:11.603
A write never goes through the read path.

49
00:03:11.603 --> 00:03:16.800
Only the runbook's publish and rollback
scripts send one, with an approved hash.

50
00:03:20.550 --> 00:03:23.175
The access check writes six kinds of row.

51
00:03:23.175 --> 00:03:27.787
Cloud: the settings name a known cloud,
and the Graph host comes from it.

52
00:03:27.787 --> 00:03:30.700
Read: the first read of incidents answers.

53
00:03:30.700 --> 00:03:34.713
Role: each designed role exists
with exactly its permissions,

54
00:03:34.713 --> 00:03:37.625
and a role that isn't in
the design is a warning.

55
00:03:37.625 --> 00:03:42.338
Restricted: advanced live response
is held only by the Tier 0 role.

56
00:03:42.338 --> 00:03:44.942
P I M: every group a
role is assigned to is

57
00:03:44.942 --> 00:03:48.362
one that Privileged Identity
Management governs.

58
00:03:48.362 --> 00:03:52.801
And emergency: no emergency access
group holds a Defender role.

59
00:03:52.801 --> 00:03:56.475
The input is a role export the
operator files from the portal.

60
00:03:56.475 --> 00:04:00.260
Learn names no API for those
roles on the pages read.

61
00:04:04.010 --> 00:04:05.060
Who's involved?

62
00:04:05.060 --> 00:04:09.035
The platform owner owns this gate:
creates and assigns the roles,

63
00:04:09.035 --> 00:04:12.410
and changes them under a change
record when a row fails.

64
00:04:12.410 --> 00:04:16.388
The security officer reviews who
holds Defender roles each quarter,

65
00:04:16.388 --> 00:04:19.598
alongside the Privileged
Identity Management report.

66
00:04:19.598 --> 00:04:22.108
And developers maintain the call list,

67
00:04:22.108 --> 00:04:25.247
the invokers and the role
check, with their tests.

68
00:04:25.247 --> 00:04:28.347
One caution: Lab 1 reads
fictional fixtures,

69
00:04:28.347 --> 00:04:31.360
and the role export is
a file someone makes.

70
00:04:31.360 --> 00:04:35.013
Treat the result as a review
aid for the security officer,

71
00:04:35.013 --> 00:04:37.790
not as the authority on who holds what.

72
00:04:41.540 --> 00:04:45.652
Lab 1 needs PowerShell 7 with
Pester, in the kit's folder.

73
00:04:45.652 --> 00:04:48.889
Step 1 runs the access
check on the fixtures.

74
00:04:48.889 --> 00:04:52.528
The read passes: five
incidents, no permission error.

75
00:04:52.528 --> 00:04:54.277
Then three rows fail.

76
00:04:54.277 --> 00:04:58.983
The detection engineer role holds
advanced live response, which only the

77
00:04:58.983 --> 00:05:03.227
Tier 0 role may hold, so both its
role row and the restricted row fail.

78
00:05:03.227 --> 00:05:06.061
And the responder role is
also assigned to a standing

79
00:05:06.061 --> 00:05:09.215
group that Privileged Identity
Management doesn't govern.

80
00:05:09.215 --> 00:05:12.590
The legacy role is a warning:
it isn't in the design,

81
00:05:12.590 --> 00:05:15.627
so someone records a
decision or removes it.

82
00:05:15.627 --> 00:05:18.050
Fourteen checks, three failed.

83
00:05:21.800 --> 00:05:24.755
Step 2 reads what each
call needs: three reads,

84
00:05:24.755 --> 00:05:29.787
each with a read permission, and two
writes with read-write custom detection.

85
00:05:29.787 --> 00:05:33.720
Step 3 reads the manifest: the
task, the time, fixture mode,

86
00:05:33.720 --> 00:05:38.081
the one call the check made with
its permission and Learn page,

87
00:05:38.081 --> 00:05:43.125
a hash for every file, and the result
Chapter 8's evidence record carries.

88
00:05:43.125 --> 00:05:45.475
Step 4 runs the chapter's tests.

89
00:05:45.475 --> 00:05:47.237
All pass, none fail.

90
00:05:47.237 --> 00:05:49.820
Delete the output folder when you're done.

91
00:05:53.570 --> 00:05:56.107
Lab 2 uses a test
tenant, never production,

92
00:05:56.107 --> 00:06:00.435
licensed for Defender for Endpoint
Plan 2, with the four roles created,

93
00:06:00.435 --> 00:06:05.658
and an account that holds Security Reader
through Privileged Identity Management.

94
00:06:05.658 --> 00:06:09.394
Sign in to Graph in the Global
environment with the read scopes.

95
00:06:09.394 --> 00:06:12.183
Run the access check
without the fixture path.

96
00:06:12.183 --> 00:06:17.008
The read row should pass with up to five
incidents, or none in a new tenant.

97
00:06:17.008 --> 00:06:19.682
An error that names a
role means the account

98
00:06:19.682 --> 00:06:22.058
lacks one of the four roles Learn lists.

99
00:06:22.058 --> 00:06:25.533
Every failing role row is a
change under a change record.

100
00:06:25.533 --> 00:06:29.530
The check changed nothing, so
backing out is just signing out.

101
00:06:33.280 --> 00:06:37.092
Here's what this gate proves,
against NIST's control catalog.

102
00:06:37.092 --> 00:06:40.512
Each role holding exactly
its designed permissions,

103
00:06:40.512 --> 00:06:44.942
with advanced live response only in
Tier 0, supports least privilege.

104
00:06:44.942 --> 00:06:47.496
Every assignment going
to a governed group,

105
00:06:47.496 --> 00:06:52.105
and no emergency access group in a Defender
role, supports account management.

106
00:06:52.105 --> 00:06:54.971
And a working read path in
the tenant's own cloud,

107
00:06:54.971 --> 00:06:59.455
with its permissions listed, supports
restricting access to audit tools.

108
00:06:59.455 --> 00:07:03.643
The indicators are FedRAMP's, as
the implementation guide maps them.

109
00:07:03.643 --> 00:07:07.830
The records support these controls;
the assessor decides them.

110
00:07:11.580 --> 00:07:12.980
What breaks in production?

111
00:07:12.980 --> 00:07:15.363
A Graph session in the wrong tenant:

112
00:07:15.363 --> 00:07:19.142
every check reads another
estate, or nothing, and passes.

113
00:07:19.142 --> 00:07:20.892
The live run refuses it.

114
00:07:20.892 --> 00:07:25.367
A permission added to a role in the
portal: someone can isolate devices

115
00:07:25.367 --> 00:07:29.918
or run live response who shouldn't;
the role and restricted rows catch it.

116
00:07:29.918 --> 00:07:35.417
A role assigned to a standing group:
privilege that never expires; the P I M row.

117
00:07:35.417 --> 00:07:38.302
An emergency access
account in a Defender role,

118
00:07:38.302 --> 00:07:41.779
which turns the break-glass
account into a daily account.

119
00:07:41.779 --> 00:07:44.199
And a write slipped into the read path:

120
00:07:44.199 --> 00:07:47.930
the read function refuses
any call marked as a write.

121
00:07:51.680 --> 00:07:53.030
Here's what done looks like.

122
00:07:53.030 --> 00:07:58.430
The settings file names GCC and the
test tenant's ID, with no secret in it.

123
00:07:58.430 --> 00:08:00.930
The read row passes in the test tenant.

124
00:08:00.930 --> 00:08:07.005
Every role, restricted, P I M and emergency
row passes, or has a change record.

125
00:08:07.005 --> 00:08:09.930
The permissions file is
filed with the role export.

126
00:08:09.930 --> 00:08:14.155
And the run folder is recorded as
evidence, which Chapter 8 shows.

127
00:08:14.155 --> 00:08:19.505
Next, Chapter 2 uses the same read path
to count the fleet against the inventory.

128
00:08:19.505 --> 00:08:20.670
Thanks for watching.
